  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nTonight through Wednesday Night...\n\nDry and quiet weather conditions will persist across our entire \nforecast area tonight as near term guidance continues to show high \npressure across the eastern CONUS. Areas west of I-55, including \nportions of southeast AR and portions of northeast LA will start to \nsee cloud cover begin to increase from west to east shortly after \nmidnight with mostly clear skies elsewhere. Overnight lows will dip \ninto the upper 40s. Another day of quiet weather is in the forecast \nfor Wednesday under mostly clear skies with daytime highs peaking \ninto the 80s. Similar conditions are expected Wednesday night with \novernight lows dipping into the upper 40s/lower 50s. /CR/\n\nThursday through Tuesday...\n\nDry conditions will continue to prevail over the southeast CONUS \nthrough the remainder of this week and into the weekend. As we head \ninto the weekend, long term guidance is beginning to show a shift to \nmore easterly low level flow starting early Friday morning. This \nshift in low level flow will result in a wedging effect around the \nAppalachians. This will cause a little dip in daytime highs across \nour CWA particularly Saturday and Sunday, with afternoon highs \npeaking in the upper 70s/lower 80s.\n\nRegarding the risk for fire wx concerns, fire weather will continue \nto be the main concern for the extended period given the combination \nof antecedent dry ground conditions/fuels, along with RHs falling in \nthe 20-30 percent range and at times falling into the teens. At this \ntime, no changes have been made to the fire wx graphic and \"Limited\" \nrisk for fire danger will continue to be advertised for our entire \narea. \n\nThe saving grace preventing a more critical situation worthy of red \nflag products will be relatively light wind each day, which should \nhelp control spread of fires that do develop. Nevertheless, caution \nshould continue to be exercised with any outdoor burning over the \nnext several days. Heed local burn bans, which continue to be added \nin new areas. If we begin to see signs in later forecasts of \nstronger wind on any upcoming days, messaging may need to be \nelevated accordingly. Quiet and dry weather will persist across the \narea heading into Tuesday. /CR/",
